/arxiv.org/abs/2610.19840)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19840\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Optimization and Control\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:GPU-Accelerated Preprocessing for Large-Scale Linear Programming\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Kaihuang Chen】, 【6†Jiawei Lu】, 【7†Defeng Sun】, 【8†Yancheng Yuan】, 【9†Guojun Zhang】, 【10†Xiangyu Zhang】, 【11†Xinyuan Zhao】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled GPU-Accelerated Preprocessing for Large-Scale Linear Programming, by Kaihuang Chen and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:GPU-accelerated first-order methods are increasingly effective for solving large-scale linear programs (LPs), yet LP preprocessing remains predominantly CPU-based and can become costly. A key challenge is that dependencies among presolve reductions and frequent modifications to sparse LP data make efficient GPU parallelization difficult. To address this challenge, we develop GPU-Preprocessor, a GPU-native LP preprocessing framework that integrates folding and presolve.\nL17: To efficiently implement folding on GPUs, we adapt a randomized partition refinement algorithm to LP folding, using sparse matrix-vector products and parallel sorting to construct stable row and column partitions. For presolve, we introduce a new piecewise-linear reformulation rule that exploits underlying convex piecewise-linear structure to obtain sparser LP representations.\nL18: We also develop a framework that combines phase scheduling with a Scan-Plan-Apply design to coordinate presolve rules and reduce sparse-matrix reconstruction overhead through batched updates. Numerical experiments on three LP benchmark sets show that GPU-Preprocessor achieves greater average problem-size reductions than PSLP and Gurobi Presolve, with SGM10 preprocessing-time speedups of up to $43.9\\times$ and $677.7\\times$, respectively.\nL19: Here, SGM10 denotes the shifted geometric mean with a 10-second shift. On the largest tested quadratic assignment problem LP relaxation, these speedups reach $126.3\\times$ and $2{,}207.9\\times$. When integrated with the GPU-accelerated LP solver HPR-LP, GPU-Preprocessor achieves SGM10 speedups of $2.5\\times$ to $5.0\\times$ in combined preprocessing and solving time over HPR-LP without preprocessing. The code is available at 【14†this https URL†github.com】.\nL20: Subjects:  | Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 90C05, 90C06, 65Y05, 68W10\nL22: Cite as:  | 【15†arXiv:2610.19840】 [math.OC]\nL23:    | (or 【16†arXiv:2610.19840v1】 [math.OC] for this version)\nL24:    | 【17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.19840†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jiawei Lu [【18†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:11:40 UTC (57 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled GPU-Accelerated Preprocessing for Large-Scale Linear Programming, by Kaihuang Chen and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【12†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【19†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【20†view license】\nL41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: math.OC\nL45: \nL46: 【21†< prev】   |   【22†next >】\nL47: \nL48: 【23†new】 | 【24†recent】 | 【25†2026-10】\nL49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: 【26†math】\nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * 【27†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL56:   * 【28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL57:   * 【29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: 